{"records":[{"id":"57j37g6kdh","title":"High-resolution photocatalytic mapping of SARS-CoV-2 spike interactions on the cell surface. Datta et al","description":"Identifying virus-host interactions on the cell surface can improve our understanding of viral entry and pathogenesis. SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the COVID-19 disease, uses ACE2 as a receptor to enter cells. Yet the full repertoire of cell surface proteins that contribute to viral entry is unknown. We developed a photocatalyst-based viral-host protein microenvironment mapping platform (ViraMap) to probe the molecular neighborhood of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein on the human cell surface. Application of ViraMap to ACE2-expressing cells captured ACE2, the established co-receptor NRP1, and several novel cell surface proteins. We systematically analyzed the relevance of these candidate proteins to SARS-CoV-2 entry by knockdown and overexpression approaches in pseudovirus and authentic infection models and identified PTGFRN and EFNB1 as bona fide viral entry factors. Our results highlight additional host targets that participate in SARS-CoV-2 infection and showcase ViraMap as a powerful platform for defining viral interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-07T06:19:10.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/57j37g6kdh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/57j37g6kdh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohsan","last_name":"Saeed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suprama","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5w9pvndgrf","title":"Ca2+ is an intracellular danger signal promoting STING activation","description":"Calcium release in host cells is essential for viral infection and survival, and mutations disrupting calcium homeostasis are associated with chronic viral infections or autoimmune diseases in patients, yet the biological significance of Ca2+ signaling on STING activation remains unclear. Here we show that cytosolic Ca2+ is sensed by host cells as an immediate-early danger signal. Viral infection-activated Rho GTPases and phospholipase C-β induce ITPR-mediated, while STING translocation induces TRPV2-mediated ER Ca2+ release, both leading to store-operated Ca2+ entry through ORAI channels. Ca2+ influx activates calmodulin (CaM)-dependent protein kinases CaMKK and CaMK1D. Activated CaMK1D phosphorylates STING allowing for TBK1 trans-autophosphorylation. Inhibiting Ca2+ signaling or mutating CaMK1D-phosphorylating STING sites impairs STING activation. Ca2+ signaling is also required for STING-mediated autophagy and lysosome biogenesis, inhibited by CaMKK or CaMK inhibitors, or by deleting CaMKK1/2 or CaMK1D. These results support a two-signal mode for STING activation in which the Ca2+-CaM-CaMKK-CaMK cascade is indispensable.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-15T15:35:17.628Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5w9pvndgrf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5w9pvndgrf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nanhai","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Han","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiyu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xurui","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanyuan","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanhao","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yilin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qifei","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Run","last_name":"Fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chenguang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luhua","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhengfan","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Phosphorylation","Autophagy","Innate Immunity","Calcium","Phospholipase C","cGAS-STING Signaling Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"53v6j8s5kp","title":"A study of A bacterial host factor confines phage localization for excluding the infected compartment through cell division. Pollak Fiyaksel et al.","description":"This dataset supports the research described in our manuscript:\n\"A bacterial host factor confines phage localization for excluding the infected compartment through cell division.\"\n\nOur hypothesis was that Bacillus subtilis actively restricts bacteriophage infection through a host-driven mechanism. The data demonstrates that the bacterial protein YjbH binds to the injected phage DNA and recruits the cell division machinery to isolate the infected region via asymmetric division, ultimately confining the spread of viral components.\n\nThe dataset includes:\n\n* Raw and processed images from fluorescence and confocal microscopy, tracking phage infection dynamics.\n* Quantifications of plaque size, lysis curves, and protein–DNA binding assays (e.g., EMSA, ddPCR).\n* Western blot data for protein expression validation.\n* Cryo-ET tomograms showing asymmetric septa formation.\n* DNA-protein interaction data, including co-IP and MS results.\n\nEach dataset is labeled by figure and panel, and includes a short description to facilitate interpretation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-19T14:47:27.575Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/53v6j8s5kp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/53v6j8s5kp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Osher","last_name":"Pollak Fiyaksel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sigal","last_name":"ben-yehuda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Microbiology","Virology","Fluorescence Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2d8wzkcn3v","title":"CELL-CHEMICAL-BIOLOGY-D-17-00243 Shimada et al.","description":"This dataset contains i) metabolomic profiling performed by Metabolon, ii) FPKM-normalized RNA-seq data performed by the Stockwell lab and Columbia Genome Center, and iii) 83 compounds identified to induce similar mechanism of lethality in cancer cell lines, for the manuscript \"Copper-binding small molecule induces oxidative stress and cell cycle arrest in glioblastoma-patient-derived cells\" written by Shimada et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-25T23:28:55.694Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2d8wzkcn3v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2d8wzkcn3v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kenichi","last_name":"Shimada","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","RNA Sequencing","Chemical Compound"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7shf34snd3","title":"Data for Synthetic lethal connectivity and graph transformer improve synthetic lethality prediction","description":"Here stores the data we used to train and evaluate our MLEC-iSL model for individual sample-specific synthetic lethality prediction.\n\nThere are two types of features used in our model: omics features and biological networks. For omics features, there are two CCLE profiles (expression and essentiality) and four cell-specific features (expression, essentiality, mutation and copy number) considered in our model. As for network features, we incorporate physical protein-protein interaction (PPI) network, genetic interaction network and pathway network.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T15:52:41.149Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7shf34snd3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7shf34snd3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kunjie","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Systems Biology","Computational Biology","Deep Learning"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h772d6cx3f","title":"SynLethDB-Recon3D-A549","description":"The datasets associated with the article “Metabolic Synthetic Lethality–Guided Drug Repurposing for Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer: An Integrated Systems Biology and Molecular Modeling Study” are provided in three separate files, each corresponding to a distinct stage of the study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-26T11:56:09.523Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h772d6cx3f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h772d6cx3f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sonia","last_name":"Sepahi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"prs6zjts7b","title":"SARS-CoV-2 variants evolve convergent strategies to remodel the host response","description":"Publication\n\"SARS-CoV-2 variants evolve convergent strategies to remodel the host response\" by Bouhaddou et al.\n\nAbstract\nSARS-CoV-2 variants of concern (VOCs) emerged during the COVID-19 pandemic. Here, we used unbiased systems approaches to study the host selective forces driving VOC evolution. We discovered VOCs evolved convergent strategies to remodel the host by modulating viral RNA and protein levels, altering viral and host protein phosphorylation, and rewiring virus-host protein-protein interactions. Integrative computational analyses revealed that although Alpha, Beta, Gamma, and Delta ultimately converged to suppress interferon-stimulated genes (ISGs), Omicron BA.1 did not. ISG suppression correlated with expression of viral innate immune antagonist proteins, including Orf6, N and Orf9b, which we mapped to specific mutations. Later Omicron subvariants BA.4 and BA.5 more potently suppressed innate immunity than early subvariant BA.1, which correlated with Orf6 levels, though muted in BA.4 by a mutation that disrupts the Orf6-nuclear pore interaction. Our findings suggest SARS-CoV-2 convergent evolution overcame human adaptive and innate immune barriers, laying the groundwork to tackle future pandemics.\n\nTables\nRNA (tables 1), peptide (table 3, 6, 7), and protein (tables 4) counts and quantitative statistical comparisons (e.g. log2 fold changes and p-values; tables 2, 5, 8) between conditions, across both host and viral genes, during infection.\nTable 1. Quantification of host RNA transcript counts in transcripts per million during infection, related to Figure 2.\nTable 2. Differential host RNA expression analysis comparing conditions during infection, related to Figures 1 and 4.\nTable 3. Quantification of host and viral proteins using abundance proteomics at the feature level, related to Figures 2 and 5.\nTable 4. Quantification of host and viral proteins using abundance proteomics at the protein level, related to Figures 2 and 5.\nTable 5. Differential host protein expression analysis comparing conditions during infection, related to Figures 1 and 4.\nTable 6. Quantification of host and viral phosphorylated peptides using phosphoproteomics at the feature level, related to Figure 2.\nTable 7. Quantification of host and viral phosphorylation sites using phosphoproteomics summarized at the site level, related to Figure 2.\nTable 8. Differential host phosphorylation site intensity analysis comparing conditions during infection, related to Figures 1 and 4.\n\nSupplemental Tables\nTable S1. Consensus mutations in the VOCs from CoVariants.org, related to Figure 1.\nTable S2. Viral RNA, protein, and phosphorylation site abundances during infection, including analysis comparing viral phosphorylated peptide intensities across VOCs, related to Figures 2 and 5.\nTable S3. Affinity purification mass spectrometry (APMS) metadata and results, related to Figure 3.\nTable S4. Integrative computational analysis metadata and results, related to Figure 4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-22T14:13:26.672Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/prs6zjts7b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/prs6zjts7b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mehdi","last_name":"Bouhaddou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nevan","last_name":"Krogan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Virus","Proteomics","Protein","Peptides","RNA Sequencing","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cbndpwhw73","title":"Frey et al Western Blot Images","description":"Original Western Blot images relevant to publication \"Targeting an essential viral oncoprotein with an IL-7-enhanced mRNA vaccine induces durable immunity to Merkel cell carcinoma.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-15T17:35:42.204Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cbndpwhw73","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cbndpwhw73"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Frey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frankie","last_name":"Scallo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeffrey","last_name":"Ishizuka","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"642gjvx74d","title":"Nonstructural protein 1 of SARS-CoV-2 is a potent pathogenicity factor redirecting host protein synthesis machinery toward viral RNA. Yuan et al","description":"Supplemental tables of \"Nonstructural protein 1 of SARS-CoV-2 is a potent pathogenicity factor redirecting host protein synthesis machinery toward viral RNA\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-19T14:44:13.43Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/642gjvx74d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/642gjvx74d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shuai","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bsmw8g6wjy","title":"Human cytomegalovirus pUS22: a novel transcriptional inhibitor that represses cellular necroptosis.","description":"Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is an important human pathogen and paradigm for subversion of host intrinsic, innate, and adaptive immunity. Necroptosis is a critical intrinsic antiviral defence that directly eliminates infected cells, preventing viral spread. However, a systematic analysis to comprehensively identify HCMV necroptosis antagonists is lacking. Here, we screened HCMV block deletion viruses lacking clusters of ‘accessory’ genes non-essential for viral replication, cell lines stably expressing individual genes in the candidate block, and single-gene deletion viral mutants. This identified pUS22 as a novel necroptosis inhibitor and the first viral protein to repress transcription of the key necrosome component RIPK3. pUS22 sequesters transcription factors Sp1 and Sp3, which would otherwise activate RIPK3 transcription by binding a core proximal promoter element. As a consequence of reduced RIPK3 protein abundance, phosphorylation of terminal necroptosis mediator MLKL is inhibited, limiting cell death and enhancing viral replication and spread. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-11T10:50:03.29Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bsmw8g6wjy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bsmw8g6wjy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hanqi","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"MR/X000516/1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"309425/Z/24/"}],"categories":["Virology","Proteomics","Cell Death","Human Cytomegalovirus"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":127041,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":133,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":127041,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":126559,"restricted":63,"embargoed":419},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3471},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2067},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":597},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":340},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":283},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":261}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":124462},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1892},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":127},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":70},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":63},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017